.TH CTPOOL_SHUTDOWN_IMMEDIATE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_shutdown_immediate \- immediate shutdown
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_shutdown_immediate(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Cancels every task that is in the queue, then tells each worker thread
to exit once its current task, if it has one, finishes. The call blocks
until every worker thread has exited.

A cancelled task that ctpool_submit(3), ctpool_try_submit(3) or
ctpool_timed_submit(3) queued with an on_complete gets
on_complete(arg, false), and its fn never runs. Those calls run on the
thread that calls this function, before it returns, with no lock of
the pool held. From such an on_complete, ctpool_wait(3) and both
shutdown functions on the same pool are no-ops, and ctpool_destroy(3)
on the same pool is a fatal error; see ctpool_submit(3).

The library marks the future of a cancelled task as done and as
cancelled, and wakes every thread that blocks in ctpool_future_get for
a cancelled future; each of them receives NULL.

A second call, whether it follows the first one or runs at the same time
on another thread, does no more shutdown work of its own, but it still
blocks until the shutdown that is already under way stops. Every caller
therefore gets the same guarantee: after the call returns, every worker
thread has exited.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed
       handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A task that is already running when you make this call finishes as
usual.
Every submit function returns ccol_not_permitted after this call, with
one exception: ctpool_submit_future, which reports a failure only as a
NULL return.

A call to this function while a ctpool_shutdown_drain already runs
escalates that drain instead of joining it unchanged: it discards
whatever is still in the queue at that moment, cancels the futures of
those tasks, and calls on_complete(arg, false) for each of them that
has one, exactly as if this call had started the shutdown. A task
that the drain already started still finishes as usual.

A call to ctpool_shutdown_immediate on pool from inside a task that runs
on one of the worker threads of pool, or from the on_complete callback
of that task, does nothing at all, for the reason that
ctpool_shutdown_drain documents for the same case: a worker thread
cannot join itself. This call neither discards the queue nor blocks.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
